# Replacing an order. A smaller size at the same price is amended in place and keeps the order's place in the
# queue. A larger size or a new price re-enters the order at the back.
> add ACME
added ACME
> buy 100 @ 100.00
ACME #1 BUY 100 @ 100.00 -> resting 100
> buy 100 @ 100.00
ACME #2 BUY 100 @ 100.00 -> resting 100

# Order 1 shrinks to 60 and stays first in line, so the next sell trades with it.
> replace 1 60 @ 100.00
ACME #1 replace with 60 @ 100.00 -> amended in place, priority kept: resting 60
> sell 60 @ 100.00
ACME #3 SELL 60 @ 100.00 -> filled 60
  trade #1  60 @ 100.00  buy #1  sell #3

# Order 2 grows to 150, which sends it behind order 4.
> buy 100 @ 100.00
ACME #4 BUY 100 @ 100.00 -> resting 100
> replace 2 150 @ 100.00
ACME #2 replace with 150 @ 100.00 -> priority reset: resting 150
> order 2
ACME #2 BUY 150 @ 100.00 GTC, sequence 5
> sell 100 @ 100.00
ACME #5 SELL 100 @ 100.00 -> filled 100
  trade #2  100 @ 100.00  buy #4  sell #5
> book
ACME  CONTINUOUS  last 100.00  resting 1
              BID                       ASK
     100.00 x 150     (1)

# A new price also resets priority. Replacing an order that isn't there is rejected.
> replace 2 150 @ 100.25
ACME #2 replace with 150 @ 100.25 -> priority reset: resting 150
> book
ACME  CONTINUOUS  last 100.00  resting 1
              BID                       ASK
     100.25 x 150     (1)
> replace 9 10 @ 100.00
ACME #9 replace with 10 @ 100.00 -> rejected: UNKNOWN_ORDER
